  "workings": {
    "bank": "Gross assets are the bank balance only (B.CA is the only debit balance-sheet line with a balance): 2024 25,000 - 17,000 = 8,000; 2025 8,000 + 22,000 - 18,000 = 12,000; 2026 12,000 + 28,000 + 5,000 - 31,000 = 14,000; 30.6.2027 14,000 + 16,000 - 16,000 = 14,000.",
    "turnover": "P.T is fed by 4000 only. The 2026 other income of 5,000 (account 4100) lands on P.OI, so turnover is 28,000, not 33,000. The closes of 2024 and 2025 are closing journals, left out of every P&L line, so turnover stays 25,000 and 22,000.",
    "shortYear": "2027-01-01 to 2027-06-30 is 181 days (31+28+31+30+31+30). The twelve months from 2027-01-01 have 365 days. Adjusted turnover limits: micro 30,000 x 181 / 365 = 14,876.7123 (shown 14,876.71); small 90,000 x 181 / 365 = 44,630.1370 (44,630.14); medium 300,000 x 181 / 365 = 148,767.1233 (148,767.12). Exact test for micro: 16,000 x 365 = 5,840,000 > 30,000 x 181 = 5,430,000, so exceeded. Unadjusted, 16,000 would be within 30,000 and the micro conditions would be met.",
    "transitional": "Set B tests the preceding year against itself for the first year it governs (2026). 2025 against B: 22,000 <= 30,000, 12,000 <= 15,000, 2 <= 3, so met as micro; 2026 is met too, so 2026 is MICRO on both years. Without that rule 2025 would be tested against A (22,000 > 20,000 and 12,000 > 10,000: not met), the years would differ, 2025's micro status (false) would carry, and 2026 would be SMALL.",
    "twoYears": "2024 meets the micro conditions (8,000 and 2 are within 10,000 and 3) but the preceding year did not (the facts say the smallest class met was SMALL), so the preceding status carries: not micro. 2025 exceeds two micro limits; 2024 met them; they differ, so 2024's micro status (false) carries again. 2027 exceeds two micro limits; 2026 met them; they differ, so 2026's micro status (true) carries: still a micro-entity."
  },
